-- Q11542: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["NRV and fair value produce the same $275,000 result because both reflect current market conditions","NRV is $250,000 (selling price minus completion and selling costs); fair value is $275,000 (the orderly transaction price)","NRV is $290,000 (the selling price alone) while fair value equals $320,000 (the historical cost basis)","Both measurements equal $290,000 because the selling price is the only relevant input for each method"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 330-10-35, NRV equals estimated selling price ($290,000) minus costs to complete ($15,000) and costs to sell ($25,000), totaling $250,000. Under ASC 820, fair value is the exit price in an orderly transaction between market participants -- the $275,000 offer. NRV is entity-specific while fair value is market-based.
Wrong (A): NRV ($250,000) and fair value ($275,000) differ because NRV deducts entity-specific costs while fair value reflects the market participant perspective.
Wrong (C): NRV deducts completion and selling costs from the selling price; historical cost is not relevant to fair value measurement.
Wrong (D): NRV requires deducting costs to complete and sell, so it cannot equal the selling price alone.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11542;

-- Q11543: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Historical cost -- the exchange price at the acquisition date","Current replacement cost -- the price to acquire an equivalent asset today","Net realizable value -- estimated selling price less completion and disposal costs","Present value of future cash flows -- the discounted amount of expected future inflows or outflows"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under SFAC No. 5 and SFAC No. 8, present value of future cash flows measures an asset or liability at the discounted amount of expected future net cash flows. This attribute is commonly applied to long-term receivables, lease liabilities under ASC 842, and pension obligations under ASC 715, explicitly incorporating the time value of money.
Wrong (A): Historical cost measures the exchange price at acquisition without considering time value of money or future cash flows.
Wrong (B): Current replacement cost reflects what it would cost to acquire an equivalent asset today, not future cash flow expectations.
Wrong (C): Net realizable value is the estimated selling price less completion and disposal costs -- an exit-based measure, not a discounted cash flow measure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11543;

-- Q11594: Financial Reporting: For-Profit [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
