-- Q11825: Not-for-Profit Accounting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recognize $200,000 as contribution revenue with donor restrictions in the current period","Recognize $200,000 as contribution revenue without donor restrictions in the current period","Record a $200,000 refundable advance liability until the funds are actually received","Do not recognize any amount because an intention to give is not an unconditional promise to give"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 958-605-25-2, contribution revenue is recognized only when an unconditional promise to give is made. A mere intention or expression of willingness to give does not constitute a legally enforceable promise and creates no asset for the NFP.
Wrong (A): No revenue can be recognized because the statement is an intention, not an unconditional promise with verifiable documentation.
Wrong (B): Same as A -- without an unconditional promise, no contribution revenue of any type is recognized.
Wrong (C): A refundable advance requires actual receipt of assets; no funds have been received by Sunrise.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11825;

-- Q11831: Not-for-Profit Accounting [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Contribution revenue with donor restrictions for the full $750,000 received","Contribution revenue without donor restrictions for the full $750,000 received","An agency liability of $735,000, net of the $15,000 processing fee earned","A refundable advance liability for the full $750,000 until funds are distributed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 958-605-25-24 through 25-27, an NFP that receives assets with instructions to transfer them to specified beneficiaries, without variance power, acts as an agent. Sycamore has no discretion, bears no risk, and the foundation retains recall rights. The $15,000 fee (2% x $750,000) is agency fee revenue; the remaining $735,000 is a liability.
Wrong (A): Sycamore acts as an agent, not a donee -- it has no variance power and cannot redirect the funds, so it does not recognize contribution revenue.
Wrong (B): Same reasoning as A; agency transactions are recorded as liabilities, not revenue.
Wrong (D): A refundable advance applies when an NFP receives conditional contributions; this is an agency arrangement with specified beneficiaries.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11831;

-- Q11836: Not-for-Profit Accounting [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$120,000 contribution revenue with $28,000 reported as fundraising expense on the statement of activities","$85,000 contribution revenue, $35,000 exchange revenue, and $28,000 cost of direct benefits to donors","$120,000 special event revenue reported gross, with $28,000 netted against it as direct costs","$85,000 contribution revenue with $28,000 reported as fundraising expense, omitting exchange revenue"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 958-225-45-5, when a special event has both a contribution element and an exchange element, each component is reported separately. The exchange portion equals the fair value of direct benefits ($35,000), while the contribution portion is the excess ($120,000 - $35,000 = $85,000). The $28,000 in direct costs is reported as cost of direct benefits to donors.
Wrong (A): This fails to separate the exchange component ($35,000) from the contribution component and misclassifies direct costs.
Wrong (C): The revenue must be split into contribution and exchange components rather than reported as a single gross amount.
Wrong (D): This correctly splits the contribution but omits the $35,000 exchange revenue component required by ASC 958-225.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11836;

-- Q11958: Debt [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recognize a $100,000 gain and reduce the carrying amount of the liability to $1,250,000","Reduce the carrying amount to $1,100,000 and recognize a gain of $150,000 in current earnings","Compute a new effective interest rate equating the $1,250,000 carrying amount to modified future payments with no gain","Record the restructured debt at fair value and recognize the difference between fair value and carrying amount as a gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 470-60-35-6, when total future cash payments under modified terms ($1,350,000) exceed the carrying amount ($1,250,000), no gain is recognized. Instead, a new effective interest rate is computed that equates the carrying amount to the present value of modified future payments, and all subsequent payments reduce the liability.
Wrong (A): A gain arises only when future cash flows are less than the carrying amount, which is not the case here.
Wrong (B): Reducing to the principal amount and recognizing a gain is incorrect when total future payments exceed the carrying amount.
Wrong (D): ASC 470-60 applies to troubled debt restructurings, not ASC 470-50 fair value extinguishment accounting.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11958;

-- Q11968: Debt [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$865, because the effective interest method applies a constant market rate to a declining carrying amount","$865, because the stated rate decreases each year as the bond premium is amortized over time","$14,420, because premium amortization fully replaces interest expense in later periods","$0, because interest expense remains constant each period under the effective interest method"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 835-30-35, the effective interest method applies the market rate (6%) to the carrying amount each period. As premium amortization reduces the carrying amount, interest expense declines: Year 1 = $125,580, Year 2 = $124,715, a decline of $865. The stated rate remains fixed at 7% for cash payment purposes.
Wrong (B): The stated rate does not change -- it remains 7% throughout the bond''s life; only the carrying amount decreases.
Wrong (C): Premium amortization is the difference between cash paid and interest expense, not a replacement for interest expense.
Wrong (D): Constant expense describes the straight-line method, not the effective interest method.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 11968;

-- Q12063: Intangible Assets [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Expense both projects totaling $1,800,000 as current-period research and development costs","Continue carrying both as indefinite-life intangible assets until the next annual impairment test","Reclassify both projects to finite-life intangible assets and begin systematic amortization","Reclassify completed Project Alpha to a finite-life intangible for amortization; write off abandoned Project Beta"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 350-30-35, IPR&D acquired in a business combination is classified as an indefinite-life intangible until the project is completed or abandoned. Upon completion, Project Alpha becomes a finite-life asset subject to amortization. Upon abandonment, Project Beta''s carrying amount is written off as an impairment loss.
Wrong (A): Acquired IPR&D is capitalized under ASC 805, not expensed as period R&D costs -- only internally generated R&D is expensed as incurred.
Wrong (B): Status changes require reclassification; indefinite-life treatment continues only while projects remain in process.
Wrong (C): The abandoned project cannot be reclassified to finite-life; it must be written off as an impairment loss.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12063;

-- Q12064: Intangible Assets [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["10% of original cost, consistent with conventions applied to tangible depreciable assets","Zero, unless a third party has committed to purchase the asset or an active market exists","Fair value at the end of useful life as estimated by management using current projections","The amount recoverable through undiscounted future cash flows over the remaining useful life"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 350-30-35-8, the residual value of a finite-life intangible asset is assumed to be zero unless a third party has committed to purchase the asset at the end of its useful life or an active exchange market exists from which a residual value can be determined.
Wrong (A): There is no default 10% residual value assumption in GAAP; the presumption for intangibles is zero.
Wrong (C): Management estimates of future fair value are not a basis for residual value; the standard requires a committed purchaser or active market.
Wrong (D): Undiscounted future cash flows relate to impairment testing under ASC 360, not residual value determination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12064;

-- Q12069: Intangible Assets [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$800,000, reported at the original capitalized development cost without adjustment","$600,000, reported at the unamortized cost before the net realizable value assessment","$200,000, the excess amount of unamortized cost over net realizable value","$450,000, because ASC 985-20 requires reporting at the lower of unamortized cost or NRV"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 985-20-35-4, capitalized software costs for external sale are reported at the lower of unamortized cost or NRV at each balance sheet date. NRV ($450,000) is below unamortized cost ($600,000), so the asset is written down to $450,000 with a $150,000 charge to cost of sales.
Wrong (A): The original cost is irrelevant after amortization has begun; reporting is based on unamortized cost compared to NRV.
Wrong (B): Unamortized cost ($600,000) exceeds NRV ($450,000), so the lower-of test requires a write-down.
Wrong (C): The $200,000 difference is the write-down amount, not the reported asset value.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12069;

-- Q12074: Intangible Assets [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$1,250,000 to each reporting unit, allocated equally regardless of relative fair values","The entire $2,500,000 to Unit A because it has the higher relative fair value","$1,675,000 to Unit A and $825,000 to Unit B, allocated based on relative fair values of each unit","Goodwill allocation is deferred until the first annual impairment test is performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 350-20-35-40, goodwill from a business combination is assigned to reporting units expected to benefit from the synergies using relative fair values. Unit A receives 67% x $2,500,000 = $1,675,000 and Unit B receives 33% x $2,500,000 = $825,000.
Wrong (A): Equal allocation ignores the relative benefit analysis required by the standard; units with higher fair values receive proportionally more goodwill.
Wrong (B): Goodwill is allocated proportionally to all benefiting units, not entirely to the unit with the highest fair value.
Wrong (D): Goodwill must be allocated to reporting units at the acquisition date, not deferred until impairment testing.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 12074;
